.TH EFS 4
.SH NAME
efs, mkefs, efsck, efsdump \- copy-on-write disk file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B disk/mkefs
[
.BI -s " startsector"
]
.BI -n " nblocks"
[
.BI -a " file"
]...
.I image
.PP
.B disk/efs
.RB [ -a | -b ]
[
.BI -s " startsector"
]
[
.BI -C " block"
]
.BI -f " device"
.I mountpoint
.PP
.B disk/efsck
[
.BI -s " startsector"
]
.RB [ -v ]
.I image
.PP
.B disk/efsdump
[
.BI -s " startsector"
]
.I image
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Efs
serves a file system out of
.I device
and mounts it on
.IR mountpoint .
Nothing committed is ever overwritten in place: a change writes new
blocks from the leaf up, and the only write that makes any of it visible
is a superblock at the end.  Until that write lands the old file system
is the file system; after it, the new one is; there is no third state.
.PP
The point of that rule is what happens after power is lost.  The contract
is
.B "crash, mount, usable"
\(em not crash, mount, check, repair, usable.  There is no recovery stage
and
.I efsck
is not one:
.I efsck
is an offline instrument that says whether what is on the disk satisfies
the format's invariants, and it never repairs anything.
.PP
.I Mkefs
makes a file system.  It runs once and it is a separate program on
purpose: there is no ream flag on the server and no file system made as a
side effect of mounting one, so a board that boots and finds no file
system says so.
.BI -n " nblocks"
is required.
.BI -s " startsector"
puts block 0 at that sector, for a partition rather than a whole file;
.I mkefs
prints the absolute sector range it is about to write before writing any
of it, because a mistake in that arithmetic overwrites whatever is next
to the partition.
.BI -a " file"
copies a host file into the root directory.
.PP
.I Efsdump
prints the superblocks, the tree and the free map.  It is the second
opinion on
.IR mkefs :
the two programs share the format library but not the code that lays a
file system out.
.PP
The options to
.I efs
are those of a file server \(em
.B -a
and
.B -b
for
.B MAFTER
and
.BR MBEFORE ,
.BI -s " startsector"
as above \(em with one exception.
.BI -C " block"
sets the block the allocator starts looking at.  It is a test
instrument, and the only way to reach the second and later blocks of the
free map without first writing 128MB; see BUGS.
.SH "ON-DISK FORMAT"
4KB blocks.  Two superblocks, at block 0 and at the last block, each with
a generation and a checksum; mount takes the newer one that checksums.
One B-tree holds all metadata, keyed by 17 bytes of inode number, key
type and offset, so an inode, a directory entry and a data extent are the
same kind of record and there is no second structure to keep consistent.
Every block carries a checksum, which turns "the tree is wrong" into
"this block is wrong".
.PP
The free map is a bitmap with one bit per block, in blocks of its own:
one 4KB block covers 32768 blocks, and the superblock names an index
block of up to 512 of those.  That is 64GB; past it the index would need
a second level, and every program here refuses rather than writing a map
it knows is short.
.PP
Blocks freed by a commit are not allocatable until the commit after it.
They are listed in the superblock that freed them and folded into the map
by the next commit.  Without that rule a crash part way through a commit
can scribble on the tree that commit was meant to fall back to.
.PP
.IR doc/efs-format.ms
is the specification and gives the reasoning.
