.SH BUGS
What is real and what is only demonstrated, because the difference
matters here more than usual.
.PP
.B Real, and tested:
everything in the six suites above, under
.I emu
on a host, against image files.  That includes a crash harness that
refuses every write in turn across a whole workload and requires the
image to mount and hold exactly the last committed state after each one,
with
.I efsck
silent; and a free map of two groups, written across the 32768-block
boundary.
.PP
.B Never run on the board.
The binaries are in the raspi3b kernel and the partition arithmetic is
printed, but no
.I efs
has been made on the card, nothing boots from one, and
.I kfs
is still what
.B raspi3binit.b
reams.
.PP
.B The allocation rate is high.
A workload whose live data is a few hundred kilobytes needs a 256MB Dis
heap.  That is garbage rather than a leak \(em
.B /dev/memheap
showed the live count falling from 41516 to 5835 within one run \(em but
it is a rate worth reducing before this runs on a board with 1GB.
.PP
.BI -C " block"
is a test hook in a production program.  It sets where the allocator
starts; it does not prove that the allocator REACHES the high groups by
filling the low ones, which nothing yet does.
.PP
An overlong
.I pendingfree
list is refused rather than spilled to a block of its own, which the
format allows for.  Nothing generates one yet.
